# A make scancode with no matching break, so the key stays HELD in the
# emulator's key set for the whole run. Under the NAK model (the default since
# main 13889) an interrupt endpoint completes only when the report differs from
# the last one delivered, and a keyboard nobody types on reports all zeros
# forever -- the very first comparison matches, so no completion ever arrives
# and kbd-check leaves the phase armed. The alternative was
# -hid-instant-complete, which would pin this test to the model that CL
# deliberately abandoned, the one where completions are plentiful enough to
# hide the starvation class usb-hid-steal exists to catch.
#
# -hid-keys routes the scancode through the USB HID model only, so the
# completion proves the interrupt-IN path rather than the PS/2 emulation.
-hid-keys
